(function(root){'use strict';
 const entries=[{"title":"Mild Fire","paragraphs":["A mild wildfire moved through this ecosystem. Some ecosystems require mild wildfires to occur regularly in order to remain healthy. This is referred to as a fire regime.","During a mild wildfire, the majority of low-lying shrubs and grasses will burn, but the majority of trees will not burn. Because less fuel is burnt, the wildfire intensity is much lower, meaning the fire doesn’t burn as hot or as quickly. Additionally, since the fuel density is cleared out, regular mild wildfires ensure that more severe wildfires do not occur.","Wildlife is often able to move out of the area during a slow-moving wildfire, returning to the area once it is safe. The low-intensity fire will go through a complete combustion reaction, producing white smoke that contains fewer carcinogens. The ashes left over will enrich the soil, supporting regrowth of plant life following the wildfire."]},{"title":"Moderate Fire","paragraphs":["A moderate wildfire occurred. This means that some areas of the forest experienced high-intensity burning, clearing out both the low-lying shrubs and the tree overstory. Other areas experienced low-intensity burning, where only the low-lying shrubs were cleared away.","Wildlife is often able to escape from a moderate wildfire, moving into areas where the fire is more mild and slow-moving. Following the wildfire, the areas that experienced low-intensity fire will still experience regrowth to support wildlife populations in that ecosystem, allowing the ecosystem to recover over time."]},{"title":"Severe Fire","paragraphs":["A severe wildfire occurred. This means that the majority of vegetation, including both low-lying shrubs and overstory trees, were burnt. This would cause a high-intensity fire throughout the ecosystem, burning hot and moving quickly. This type of fire tends to result in incomplete combustion reactions, producing black smoke that contains many carcinogens. Additionally, this type of fire is the most likely to affect human settlements, as it is difficult to control the spread of the fire.","There is little regrowth following a high-intensity fire, so the habitat will not be able to support wildlife. The soil itself will go through a chemical change, causing it to be hydrophobic, meaning that water will pool on the surface; this can cause dirt and debris to runoff into the watershed.","Severe fires have become much more common over the last 100 years due to several factors. There have been changes in temperature and precipitation patterns, which increase the likelihood that a fuel source will ignite during a fire. Additionally, humans have suppressed even mild wildfires, allowing fuel to build up significantly.","Scientists are currently implementing several strategies to mitigate severe wildfire. See the U.S. Forest’s Service’s Fire Management page for more information on mitigation."]}];
 function classify(percent){return entries[percent<25?0:percent<=75?1:2];}
 const api={classify};if(typeof module!=='undefined'&&module.exports)module.exports=api;else root.FuelSeverity=api;
})(typeof window!=='undefined'?window:globalThis);
